UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 2: Group dance patterns', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters. A pathway is the line each dancer draws on the floor, and in a group they can cross, follow or split. A canon is when dancers do the same movement but start one after another, like a round in music. Good spacing means keeping an arm''s length between dancers so no one bumps. The dance looks stronger when all dancers face the same direction at the right moment.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a group dance pattern for three dancers using a line, a circle and a canon. Describe what each formation looks like.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Start in a straight line. Move into a circle by walking in curves. Do a three-count phrase as a canon, starting one after another.'' A change of formation and a canon make the pattern interesting while staying safe with spacing.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast. Clear spacing and a planned pattern are safer and easier to see.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPlan a group pattern with at least three formations and one canon. Draw an overhead map with arrows for each dancer''s pathway. Rehearse with family or practise by yourself and draw stick figures for each formation.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters. A pathway is the line each dancer draws on the floor, and in a group they can cross, follow or split. A canon is when dancers do the same movement but start one after another, like a round in music. Good spacing means keeping an arm''s length between dancers so no one bumps. The dance looks stronger when all dancers face the same direction at the right moment.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Plan a group pattern with at least three formations and one canon. Draw an overhead map with arrows for each dancer''s pathway. Rehearse with family or practise by yourself and draw stick figures for each formation. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a formation on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Start in a straight line.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast. Clear spacing and a planned pattern are safer and easier to see. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a formation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a formation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a formation with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a formation. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a formation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Start in a straight line.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"Plan a group dance pattern for three dancers using a line, a circle and a canon. Describe what each formation looks like. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a formation; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Start in a straight line.","Careful use of a canon and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.","In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a formation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a canon and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply a formation to new material and name the a canon and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.","Name a canon and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a formation."],"question":"Which response best applies a formation with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on a formation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Start in a straight line.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for a formation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for a formation."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a formation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Example: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for a formation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a canon and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Plan a group pattern with at least three formations and one canon.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast."],"question":"Which independent response best applies a formation to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a formation with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a formation on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a formation on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a formation. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a formation, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Start in a straight line.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w33-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a formation.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use a formation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a canon and explain why it matters.","I can practise a pathway independently on a new prompt.","I can notice spacing needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters.","options":["In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a formation with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later, then Dancer 3 and Dancer 4. They begin in a straight line, move into a circle and end in a cluster. They keep an arm''s length apart and watch the leader''s count to keep in time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a group dance pattern for three dancers using a line, a circle and a canon. Describe what each formation looks like.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Start in a straight line. Move into a circle by walking in curves. Do a three-count phrase as a canon, starting one after another.'' A change of formation and a canon make the pattern interesting while staying safe with spacing.","explain":"In group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters. A pathway is the line each dancer draws on the floor, and in a group they can cross, follow or split. A canon is when dancers do the same movement but start one after another, like a round in music. Good spacing means keeping an arm''s length between dancers so no one bumps. The dance looks stronger when all dancers face the same direction at the right moment.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast. Clear spacing and a planned pattern are safer and easier to see. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Group formations.","caption":"Change formation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Line</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dancers side by side</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Circle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">curved pathways</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a formation with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later, then Dancer 3 and Dancer 4. They begin in a straight line, move into a circle and end in a cluster. They keep an arm''s length apart and watch the leader''s count to keep in time. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A canon and spacing.","caption":"Start in turn.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Canon</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">start one after another</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">an arm length apart</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a formation with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast. Clear spacing and a planned pattern are safer and easier to see. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later, then Dancer 3 and Dancer 4. They begin in a straight line, move into a circle and end in a cluster. They keep an arm''s length apart and watch the leader''s count to keep in time.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Start in a straight line. Move into a circle by walking in curves. Do a three-count phrase as a canon, starting one after another.'' A change of formation and a canon make the pattern interesting while staying safe with spacing.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that the best group dance has dancers close together and moving fast. Clear spacing and a planned pattern are safer and easier to see. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a formation with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn group dance, dancers make patterns using formations such as lines, circles, diagonals or clusters. A pathway is the line each dancer draws on the floor, and in a group they can cross, follow or split. A canon is when dancers do the same movement but start one after another, like a round in music. Good spacing means keeping an arm''s length between dancers so no one bumps. The dance looks stronger when all dancers face the same direction at the right moment. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 2: Group dance patterns","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Dance: telling stories through movement","week":33,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: a group of four plans a phrase in a canon. Dancer 1 starts: step, turn, reach. Dancer 2 starts two counts later, then Dancer 3 and Dancer 4. They begin in a straight line, move into a circle and end in a cluster. They keep an arm''s length apart and watch the leader''s count to keep in time.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Group formations.","caption":"Change formation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Line</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">dancers side by side</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Circle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">curved pathways</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A canon and spacing.","caption":"Start in turn.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Canon</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">start one after another</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">an arm length apart</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w33-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
